course: dictionary
lesson: 5861
title: Calling someone in another language
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Mamo!» is not an English word. It is Ukrainian, and it is how a child calls their mum. You say it as MAH-mo. Mamo is the calling form of «mama», which means mother.
explain: Ukrainian changes the end of a word when you call someone. This is called the vocative. «Mama» becomes «mamo», «tato» becomes «tatu», and «babusya» becomes «babusyu». Once you see the pattern, you can call anyone the right way.
explain: You can often guess a new word from the words around it. «Mamo, dyvys!» has the word «dyvys» after it, and the phrase tells you a child is calling their mum to look at something. You do not need to know every word to follow the meaning.
fact: «Mamo!» is how you call your mum in Ukrainian. It means «Mum!» or «Mom!».
fact: «Mamo» comes from «mama», which means mother.
fact: The calling form changes the end of a name: «mama» becomes «mamo», «tato» becomes «tatu», and «babusya» becomes «babusyu».
fact: «Mamo, dyvys!» means «Mum, look!» and «Mamo, ya tut» means «Mum, I'm here».
fact: «Mamo» is not an English word. You will only hear it in Ukrainian speech.
exercise: What does «Mamo!» mean? | Teacher, listen | Grandma, look | *Mum! | Dad, come here
exercise: The calling form of «tato» (dad) is: | tato | tatko | babusyu | *tatu
exercise: «Mamo, ya tut» means: | *Mum, I'm here | Mum, where are you? | Dad, I'm here | Mum, look!
test: What is the calling form of «babusya» (grandma)? | babusyo | babusi | babusya | *babusyu
test: «Mamo, dyvys!» means: | Mum, I'm here | Mum, wait! | Mum, listen! | *Mum, look!
exam: What is the calling form of «titka» (aunt)? | *titko | titka | titkoo | titku
